PSPATTERN 0.2.3a

    FM: the operator sine is interpolated now.
    Every operator read the nearest of 1024 table entries and threw
        away the other 22 bits of phase. Measured on a bare operator
        with nothing modulating it, everything that was not the
        fundamental sat at -55 dB, which matched a no-interpolation
        reference to a tenth of a decibel.
    It matters more in FM than anywhere else in the synth, because a
        modulator's impurity is multiplied into the carrier's spectrum
        rather than just added to the output. Two operator FM had
        -44 dB of energy sitting between the partials where the Bessel
        spectrum says there should be none.
    Now interpolated against a step table, the way msfa does it, so the
        lookup is a multiply and an add. Bare operator -89 dB, two
        operator FM -77 dB between the partials: 34 dB and 33 dB
        better. Costs 4 points at eight voices of four operator FM,
        47 to 51 per cent of a block.

    FM filter fix, a regression from 0.2.2a.
    That release made the SVF tick twice per sample in the TONE and VAX
        engines and rebuilt the shared coefficient table for the
        doubled rate. The FM engine has the same filter and was missed,
        so it was reading half rate coefficients into a single pass and
        every FM patch's filter sat an octave below where its cutoff
        said it was. All three engines tick twice now.

PSPATTERN 0.2.2a

    The oscillators are band limited now.
    Saw and pulse were naive: they stepped from one sample to the next
        without regard for where inside the sample the edge fell, which
        scatters energy off the harmonics and across the spectrum.
        Measured on a rendered note, energy at frequencies that are not
        in the note ran at -30 dB at A2 and -16 dB at A5, worsening
        3 dB an octave upward. That matched a naive reference exactly,
        which is what confirmed there was no band limiting at all.
    Now a two sample PolyBLEP residual at each discontinuity, on the
        main oscillator, on every member of the unison stack and on the
        sub. Same measurement after: -67 dB at A2 and -32 dB at A5, so
        16 dB better across the range and 37 dB at the bottom.
    The divide only runs inside the edge window, which is twice a
        cycle. Cost at eight voices: tone 17 to 18 per cent of a
        block, vax 25 to 30 with the filter change below.

    The filter is a real filter across its whole range.
    A Chamberlin SVF is only stable while its coefficient stays well
        under 2, which capped the table at 7.6 kHz and then clamped.
        A clamped coefficient is not a corner frequency: measured,
        cutoff 255 with resonance was not a 7.6 kHz lowpass but nearly
        flat to 15 kHz, with the warp near the stability limit standing
        in for an open filter. The top third of the knob did almost
        nothing.
    The SVF now ticks twice per sample, which halves the rate it sees
        and buys the headroom to make the range real. Table extended to
        30 Hz - 12.5 kHz. Measured after: cutoff 255 is -3 dB at
        15 kHz, cutoff 192 at 3520 Hz with -32.9 dB at 10 kHz where it
        used to be -21.2, and no resonant hump.
    Cutoff numbers now mean different frequencies than they did. Every
        setting is somewhat brighter than before, which is the safer
        direction, but existing patches are worth a listen.

    DEMO-2 level 0.29 to 0.26. A PolyBLEP residual overshoots slightly
        at each edge and the filter passes more top, which together
        took the render from no clipped samples to 84. Now 11 in 5.7
        million, all of them transient peaks in the four full
        arrangement rows.

PSPATTERN 0.2.1a

    Rendering to wav did not stop when the song stopped.
    Tearing the render down lived in the song screen's START handler, so
        it only ran when somebody pressed START a second time. A song
        that reaches its end with repeat on "once" stops on its own and
        never went through it, so the screen went on saying it was
        rendering over a file the mixer had already closed.
    Worse, the mixer stayed in stereo render mode, so the next START
        opened a fresh writer on the same path. Opening truncates, so a
        finished render was replaced by however much of the new take had
        been written. A 65 second wav came back as 0.78 seconds.
    Now handled where the player's stop event arrives, so it works from
        any screen, and it forces a redraw: a notification is painted
        into the character grid, and once playback stops nothing repaints
        the row it sits on.

    Changing an instrument's type asked "settings are lost" on
        instruments that had no settings.
    The prompt asked IsEmpty, which is the question the save code asks.
        Synth and midi instruments answer "never empty" there because
        they always have to be written to the file, so both always
        warned, including a patch straight out of the box. New projects
        assign a drum to the first instruments automatically, so those
        warned too.
    There is now a separate question for it: has anything been changed
        from the values a new instrument of this type starts with. The
        sample a new project assigns for you and the midi channel the
        bank stamps in by index are not counted, because neither was
        chosen by anybody.

    DEMO-2, a second demo song. Melody led rather than arpeggio led,
        with one arp channel instead of three, and a chord channel that
        runs the same ARPG command at a slower ARPS so it strums instead
        of trilling. Peak -0.09 dBFS, no clipped samples.

PSPATTERN 0.2.0a

    Engine fixes:
    A note that followed another note on the same channel was thrown
        away one audio block after it started. PlayerChannel set its
        releasing_ flag when stopping the old note and nothing cleared
        it, so the next Render read it as "the release finished" and
        dropped the instrument. Every note after the first on any patch
        with a release, cut about 20ms in.
    Note-on declick used the wrong correction. It added the previous
        sample instead of measuring the step, which for a square left
        nearly a full scale jump. Measured at 18.4% of full scale on a
        square, 16.6% on a pulse; now 0.00% on both.
    The declick bound could not cancel the step it measured. Capped at
        half full scale while a loud square swings 57%.
    Songs no longer loop by default.
    The low battery warning inverted once per repaint on a panel that
        repaints every frame, so it strobed instead of blinking. Now on
        the wall clock at 2Hz, with hysteresis on the threshold.
    The project selector listed the program's own samplelib folder.

    Additions:
    Velocity column in the phrase screen, reading 00..99 rather than
        hex. Empty means full, so older projects are unchanged.
    Hard sync and ring modulation on the VAX engine.
    ARPS: arp speed, in ticks per arpeggio position. Default 1 is the
        classic every-tick trill; higher values slow the figure until
        each note in it can be heard as a pitch.
    MANUAL.txt.
    Channel faders are written by the song generators, so a demo can
        carry its own mix balance.

    Interface:
    Instrument values read as decibels, per cent and L/CTR/R instead of
        raw hex where the raw number meant nothing.
    Four strings named buttons the PSP does not have.
    The boot and browser screens named the upstream project.


    Migrate W32 to SDL2, removing need for building in vs2008

